title: 'Speaker verification calibrated: 0/284 human impostors after the pause-run gate, but ~50% same-speaker rejection; ambient voice cannot rely on it'
summary: |-
  Calibration on the local human data (only 2 genuinely human speakers; VoiceStudio wavs are TTS):
  - The previous 30 dB "speech-only" gate made things WORSE (it chopped consonants).
  - Chosen pause-run gate: quiet = below max(-55 dBFS, peak-25 dB); cut the edges plus inner quiet runs ≥200 ms. Padding-invariant (cos 1.000).
  - At threshold 0.65 with ≥1500 ms of gated speech: human impostors accepted 0/284 (plain, -65 and -50 padding), synthetic 6–10/80, but same-speaker rejected ~52% (mostly the drifting long narration).
  - Residuals: -40 dBFS pause noise defeats the gate (156/284); inner gaps 37/284; utterances under 1.5 s are unreliable.
  Implication: under auto-edits, ambient speech acts as typed input, yet the speaker check either over-accepts (old) or under-accepts (new). It is a convenience filter, not a boundary.
  Founder decision requested on ambient semantics.
  Code WIP: 82014480 (gate, thresholds, enrol minimum, decode priority).
symbols_touched:
  - '#gnomon-voice'
learnings:
  - Energy gates must preserve consonants; gate on long quiet runs, not per-frame dB.
  - With two human speakers there is no real FAR estimate; design the product so a misidentification is harmless.
